// @test: run
// @tier: 0
// @level: L0
// @tags: ffi, out-param, struct, abi, differential, regression
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-exit: 0
//
// AN OUT-PARAMETER STRUCT COMES BACK FILLED — TIER 0 (T1403).
//
// This is the half that PASSES today. It exists so that a future change
// to Tier 0's marshalling cannot quietly break the behaviour that is
// currently correct: `runtime.rs` packs the Verum object into a C buffer
// and copies the callee's writes back, and that is what makes the
// numbers below land where C says they should.
//
// The Tier-1 half lives beside this file, suffixed `_aot`, and carries
// the `@skip` that names the open defect.
//
// A C function that reports through a pointer parameter — `clock_gettime`,
// `stat`, `getsockname`, `waitpid`, every `*_r` variant — writes into the
// caller's storage. This spec asks the only question that matters at that
// boundary: after the call, does the caller SEE those bytes?
//
// MEASURED 2026-09-10, before the fix:
//
//     tier 0    a=1789077992  b=234076  c=0  tv_sec=0  tv_usec=0
//     tier 1    a=0           b=0       c=0  tv_sec=0  tv_usec=0
//
// and `a` matched `int(time.time())` exactly on the same second.
//
// Tier 0 is CORRECT there and Tier 1 is silently empty. The call returns
// `rc=0` at both tiers, and nothing is printed, logged or raised — the
// program simply reads back the zeros it started with.
//
// WHY THE THREE DUMMY FIELDS. A Verum heap object is a 24-byte header
// followed by its field slots (the OBJECT_HEADER_SIZE constant in
// verum_common's layout module, which carries a drift contract).
// Tier 1 handed the callee the object's BASE, so `gettimeofday` wrote its
// 16 bytes over the header — the type id and the size — and no field was
// touched. With only two fields a fix that offset by the wrong amount could
// still land the seconds in `tv_sec` and look green. With three fields
// ahead of them, ONLY an offset of exactly one header puts the seconds in
// `a`; any other offset puts them somewhere this spec names out loud.
//
// WHAT THE FIELDS MEAN. C writes `struct timespec { tv_sec; tv_nsec }` at
// the pointer's offset 0, so the seconds land in the FIRST field of the
// record and the nanoseconds in the second — whatever those fields are
// called. `a` and `b` carry them; `tv_sec`/`tv_usec` are named that way
// deliberately, to stay ZERO, because a spec that let the C payload land in
// a field whose name matches would be pinning a coincidence.
//
// The assertion is on SHAPE and a FLOOR, not on a reading: the epoch second
// cannot be below the floor asserted, and microseconds are below one
// million by definition. A spec that pinned the
// literal reading would fail every second of every day.

mount core.io.println;

@ffi("libSystem.B.dylib")
extern {
    // `int gettimeofday(struct timeval *tp, void *tzp)`.
    //
    // NOT `clock_gettime`, and the reason is a trap this spec must not
    // fall into. `crates/verum_codegen/src/llvm/syscall_registry.rs`
    // redirects three names to Verum's own wrappers at Tier 1 —
    // `clock_gettime` to `__verum_clock_gettime` among them — so a spec
    // written on that name compares libSystem at Tier 0 against a Verum
    // wrapper at Tier 1 and calls the difference a tier divergence.
    // `gettimeofday` is in neither table, so both tiers call the same
    // libSystem symbol and the comparison means what it says.
    // The Verum name is prefixed so it cannot collide with a mounted
    // `gettimeofday`; `@ffi_name` supplies the C symbol dlsym must find.
    // Without it the run dies with
    //     symbol 'spec_gettimeofday' not found in library
    // which is at least a LOUD failure — the spec cannot silently
    // measure nothing.
    @ffi_name("gettimeofday")
    public fn spec_gettimeofday(tp: &mut Wide, tzp: Int) -> Int32;
}

public type Wide is { a: Int, b: Int, c: Int, tv_sec: Int, tv_usec: Int };

fn main() {
    let mut w = Wide { a: 0, b: 0, c: 0, tv_sec: 0, tv_usec: 0 };

    let rc = unsafe { spec_gettimeofday(&mut w, 0) };

    // PRINT BEFORE ASSERTING. `assert_eq` reports only that two values
    // differ and never shows them, so a failing run would say the struct
    // is wrong without saying what came back — and "all zeros" versus
    // "the right bytes at the wrong offset" are different defects that
    // must not look alike in the log.
    println(f"rc={rc} a={w.a} b={w.b} c={w.c} tv_sec={w.tv_sec} tv_usec={w.tv_usec}");

    assert_eq(rc, 0);

    // The callee's 16 bytes land at C offset 0 — the first two fields.
    // `a` is the epoch SECOND: an exact, externally checkable number
    // (`date +%s`), not merely "nonzero". 1789077992 was the reading
    // when this spec was written; the bound below is a floor that a
    // clock cannot fall under, so the spec never needs updating.
    assert(w.a > 1700000000);
    assert(w.b >= 0);
    assert(w.b < 1000000);           // tv_usec is MICROseconds

    // ...and nowhere else. These three sit past the timespec and must be
    // untouched; if the seconds appear HERE, the offset is wrong by
    // exactly the amount this spec exists to catch.
    assert_eq(w.c, 0);
    assert_eq(w.tv_sec, 0);
    assert_eq(w.tv_usec, 0);
}
